      "evidence": "Standard historiographical practice compares revolutions by casualty counts; alternative meanings (e.g., political impact) are not relevant to a question about deadliness.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Several other European revolutions caused far higher death tolls than the Carnation Revolution.",
      "evidence": "French Revolution (1793‑1794): ~40,000–50,000 executions plus war casualties; Russian Revolution and Civil War (1917‑1922): 5–9 million deaths; Hungarian Revolution (1956): ~2,500 deaths; Yugoslav wars (1990s, though later, still European): >100,000 deaths.",
      "weight": 0.30,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Even if the definition of \"deadliest\" were broadened to include indirect or later war casualties, the Carnation Revolution’s death count remains orders of magnitude lower than those of the revolutions listed in step 3.",
      "evidence": "The maximum plausible total deaths linked to the Carnation Revolution (including any subsequent political violence) is well under 200, far below the tens of thousands or millions of other cases.",
